What is Float Glass?
Float glass represents the foundation of modern glazing solutions, produced by floating molten glass on a bed of molten tin to create perfectly flat, optically clear panels with uniform thickness. This breakthrough manufacturing process, commercialized in the 1950s, revolutionized architectural and industrial applications by delivering distortion-free surfaces ideal for windows, facades, and specialized uses. While primarily composed of soda-lime silica, manufacturers produce specialized variants including low-iron glass for solar applications and borosilicate for enhanced thermal resistance.
The material's versatility enables critical functions across industries - from hurricane-resistant laminated glass in coastal constructions to ultra-thin flexible displays in consumer electronics. Recent advancements in coating technologies have further expanded its capabilities, allowing buildings to achieve net-zero energy targets through intelligent light and heat management.
